Answer: Implicit type casting is the automatic conversion of a value from a smaller data type to a larger compatible data type. Example:

```java
byte a = 10;
short b = a;
```

The conversion happens automatically, so an explicit cast is not required.

Answer: Explicit type casting is the conversion of a value from a larger data type to a smaller data type by explicitly specifying the target type. Example:

```java
short a = 120;
byte b = (byte) a;
```

The target type is written inside parentheses.

Answer: ```java
public class Main {
public static void main(String[] args) {
byte a = 10;
short b = a;
System.out.println(a);
System.out.println(b);
}
}
```

Sample Input: No input Output: 10 10 The byte value is automatically converted to short through implicit widening.

